<header><p class="enc-kicker">Religion / idea · deity</p><h1 class="enc-title">Elagabal</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Elagabal was the sun god of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/homs">Emesa</a>, modern Homs, in Roman <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/syria">Syria</a>, worshipped in the form of a black conical stone in a great temple served by the priestly kings of the Emesene dynasty. The emperor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/elagabalus">Elagabalus</a>, a hereditary priest of the cult, brought the stone to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> around 219 and made the god the head of the Roman pantheon, but the cult was reversed after his murder in 222.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/elagabal.jpg" alt="Elagabal" width="320" height="149" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Panairjdde ? · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Bronze-Uranius_Antoninus-Elagabal_stone-SGI_4414.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
